# ── Secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AGENTS.md §3: no secrets in the repo, ever. .env.example ships with empty keys.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ── PHP / Laravel ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
vendor/
node_modules/
/backend/public/build
/backend/public/hot
/backend/public/storage
/backend/storage/*.key
.phpunit.result.cache
/backend/.phpunit.cache

# ── Python / ml-service ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# ── Model artefacts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Trained weights are large and regenerable from training/train_*.py.
# The JSON written beside them (metrics.json, feature_list.json, the split
# indices) is the evidence behind the Chapter 4 numbers and MUST stay tracked —
# see AGENTS.md §6 and the ml-endpoint skill. Ignore weights by extension rather
# than ignoring models/ wholesale, so that metadata is never silently dropped.
*.bin
*.safetensors
*.joblib
*.pkl
*.pt
*.onnx
*.h5
ml-service/models/**/checkpoint-*/

# ── The two SERVING artefacts are the exception ────────────────────────────
# A deployment needs weights, and neither route to getting them there works by
# default: the training corpus is not committed either (see below), so a clean
# checkout can neither load a model nor train one. Railway deploys from a
# repository, so the serving weights have to be in it.
#
# Committed only because they now fit. `joblib.dump(..., compress=3)` takes the
# URL forest from 131.5 MB to 38 MB -- uncompressed it was over GitHub's hard
# 100 MB per-file limit and the push would simply have been rejected.
#
# Named individually, never by a wildcard. There is 1.4 GB of retired weights
# beside these (rf-url-v1.0.0 alone is 432 MB), and they are regenerable
# evidence rather than something a deployment needs.
!ml-service/models/rf-url-v2.0.0-pathnorm/model.joblib
!ml-service/models/tfidf-msg-v3.0.0-collected-my/model.joblib

# Local safety copies taken before re-dumping an artefact. Git is not a backup
# for a file git has never tracked -- this project learned that once already.
/.backups/

# ── Datasets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Raw corpora are not committed: size, plus the provenance/consent basis that
# AGENTS.md requires for every collected Malaysian message. Record each source
# in ml-service/training/DATASETS.md instead. Small fixtures may be committed
# with a _sample suffix.
ml-service/training/data/
*.csv
!*_sample.csv

# The collection workbook holds the same collected messages in a spreadsheet,
# including who received each one and when. Same reason as above: real messages
# sent to real people do not belong in version control. Back it up separately.
/scamsense_corpus_collection.xlsx
~$*.xlsx

# ── OS / editor ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
/.idea
/.vscode

# ── Local notes and report drafts ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Credentials kept locally while developing. Never commit: a secret in git
# history stays there after the file is deleted.
/ID Pass.txt

# The report itself. A binary .docx merges badly and would bloat the repo;
# it lives with the author, not in version control. Matched by extension, not
# by name: a file-by-file rule let two renamed chapter drafts slip into a commit.
# Report drafts, reference PDFs and screenshots live in ../ScamSense-Report/.
*.docx
*.pdf
~$*
/example wireframe.png

# Generated design canvas. Built from the .dc.html sources in docs/wireframes/
# by the design skill's seeder; each rebuild is a fresh 2.2 MB blob, so
# tracking it would grow the repository every time a figure changes.
/docs/wireframes/scamsense-chapter-4-wireframes.html

# Agent instructions and session handoff notes: kept on the development machine, not published
.claude/
SKILLS.md
docs/HANDOFF.md
